malloc() and free() 

typedef struct
{
    int health;
} Enemy;

Enemy *enemy = malloc(sizeof(Enemy));

if (enemy != NULL)
{
    enemy->health = 100;
}


Answer these:

A.

Where is enemy itself stored conceptually?

B.

Where is the actual Enemy object allocated by malloc()?

C.

What happens if we do:

free(enemy);
D.

After free(enemy), can we safely do:

enemy->health = 50;

Why or why not?


ANS :

enemy is the pointer variable. Since it's a local variable, it is typically on the stack.

The actual Enemy object created by malloc() is on the heap. 

The heap allocation is released and is no longer yours to access.

The memory has been freed, so accessing it is undefined behavior.  [The pointer variable enemy may still contain the old address, but that address no longer refers to a valid allocation owned by your program. ]


